            <div xml:id="d466">

                <pb facs="0472" n="460" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg460"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.85/274: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-02T21:00:00+02:00">April 2, 1941—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 3—6:16 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>448. Department’s 218, March 30. The substance of the Department’s telegram under
                    reference was given orally this morning to Prunas, Chief of the American Section
                    at the Foreign Office. He said that in so far as he knew the Foreign Office had
                    not yet received a full report from the Italian Embassy in Washington on the
                    matter and that Ambassador Colonna had been instructed merely to inform the
                    State Department that Italy “reserved her rights” pending further investigation.
                    While he seemed unhappy and concerned because of the action of the American
                    authorities Prunas made no further observations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
